::Q1::What is an S3 object? 
{
~An RDS database engine~An EC2 virtual machine
=A stored item consisting of data and associated metadata identified by a key
~An IAM user
}

::Q2::Why is S3 commonly used for data-lake-style storage? 
{
~It requires every file to use the same relational schema
~It stores only SQL tables
=It can store large amounts of varied data as objects and is designed for scalable storage
~It is primarily a compute service
}

::Q3::Why might a pipeline organize S3 objects by date? 
{
~To eliminate the need for permissions
=To provide a predictable structure for locating and managing time-based data
~To convert S3 into a relational database
~To change the storage service into EC2
}

::Q4::What can an application do with S3 from Python? 
{
~Only execute SQL against S3
~Only create EC2 CPU cores
~Only manage IAM passwords
=Interact with buckets and objects through an AWS SDK
}

::Q5::What is an important security practice for S3 buckets? 
{
~Storing credentials in object names
=Avoiding unintended public access
~Making every bucket public
~Disabling all access controls
}